diff --git a/LOGBOOK.md b/LOGBOOK.md
index 84c2337..d4365b3 100644
--- a/LOGBOOK.md
+++ b/LOGBOOK.md
@@ -5,6 +5,18 @@
 
 ## 2026-09-02
 
+### 2322 — One regex, two schemas, two opposite right answers
+
+- ROOT CAUSE: `semverPattern` in `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go` was applied to `EnvironmentTuple.adapter_version`, a member the pinned SPEC v0.5.0 declares with NO type and NO format. The SemVer word reaches it only through the Session Adapter Manifest row at `SPEC.md:3610`, which closes a different object, and the Probe sentence at `:3625` that equates "adapter version" to the verified Manifest — which names the Probe's own top-level member, not the nested tuple member. Cross-schema inference by field-name similarity, which §1.6 forbids. `1.2.3-rc.1` was refused with nothing in the contract rejecting it.
+- FINDING: the same compiled regex was ALSO used at `validateMigrationExtensionObject` and `validateTerminalV4Payload`, where §17.3 and §1871-1872 DO declare `semver`. So the fix is not one answer applied twice; it is one rule producing opposite answers. The constraint applies exactly where the document declares it — nowhere at the tuple, everywhere it says `semver`.
+- FINDING: at the sites where it IS declared, the pattern was wrong in the other direction. `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\.(...)\.(...)$` is not Semantic Versioning 2.0.0; it omits prerelease and build metadata entirely. The document names Semantic Version and spells out no grammar, so the only defensible reading is the whole standard. `internal/config/validation.go:21` already carried the full SemVer 2.0.0 spelling — the repo was internally inconsistent about its own type name.
+- DECISION: constraint REMOVED at the tuple (presence-only, matching its untyped `environment_version` and `store_schema_fingerprint` siblings in the same clause); constraint KEPT and WIDENED to SemVer 2.0.0 in full everywhere the document types a member `semver`. Both halves written into `internal/canonicaljson/testdata/constraint-enumeration.md` as an explicit decision section with the §17.3 authorising line quoted verbatim, not left implied by the code.
+- FINDING: widening the pattern cost 22 grammar-inventory dimensions (2 anchors, 18 character classes, 2 one-or-more quantifiers), each needing a witness that production refuses and that exactly its own one-dimension widening admits. Derived mechanically with a throwaway reimplementation of `deriveGrammarSites` rather than guessed. That inventory is the reason this was affordable at all: it forced every new class to be proven instead of shipping unpinned.
+- EVIDENCE: 4 mutants, all killed. A2 is the exact reported bug — re-adding the original core-triple gate reddens the `1.2.3-rc.1` case at `session_record_versions_test.go:1038`. B (narrow the pattern back) reddens 5 accept cases; C (delete the migration gate) reddens 8 refuse cases, so widening is provably not the same as deleting. D (drop the member from `requireExactMembers`) reddens the absence case.
+- ANOMALY: mutant D was GREEN on the first attempt. The absence case deleted `adapter_version` from `source_environment` only, and the surviving member in the sibling `target_environment` produced an unrelated extra-member refusal — the test passed for the wrong reason. Fixed by deleting from BOTH tuples and attributing the refusal by name. A refusal that fires is not evidence that YOUR clause fired.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go` (production), `grammar_inventory_test.go`, `member_shape_refusal_test.go`, `session_record_versions_test.go`, `testdata/constraint-enumeration.md`, `README.md`.
+- STATUS: `internal/canonicaljson` 97.2% before and after, verified against a `git archive HEAD` baseline. gofmt, `go build ./...`, both cross-compile targets, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `-cover -count=1`, `-race -count=1`, tracecheck, `cataloggen -check` and all five fuzz gates at 100x exit 0.
+
 ### 2237 — A fault suite that only proves the call failed proves the cheap half
 
 - FINDING (review round 2, blocking): seven fault boundaries were pinned at 2204, but only three carried a recovery half. Staged mode drift, both FIFO shard cases and BOTH quarantine-ENOSPC subtests asserted the failure state and stopped. Anything can fail; what a durability suite exists to establish is what a later run can still do. Both quarantine-ENOSPC cases are full-disk cases, so this was a direct miss against the front-loaded rule that full-disk and short-write cases must show the store is left recoverable.
